{"name":"Crooked River","hex":"#797869","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Crooked River","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A locality in the Shire of Wellington, south eastern Victoria, Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Crooked_River"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crooked River (New South Wales)","blocks":[{"text":"The Crooked River, officially designated as a creek, is an open mature wave dominated barrier estuary located in the Illawarra region of New South Wales, Australia."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crooked_River_(New_South_Wales)","wikidata":"Q14934792"},{"title":"Crooked River (Victoria)","blocks":[{"text":"The Crooked River is a perennial river of the Mitchell River catchment, in the Alpine region of the Australian state of Victoria."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crooked_River_(Victoria)","wikidata":"Q16890713"},{"title":"Crooked River (Oregon)","blocks":[{"text":"The Crooked River is a tributary, 125 miles (201 km) long, of the Deschutes River in the U.S. state of Oregon. The river begins at the confluence of the South Fork Crooked River and Beaver Creek in southeastern Crook County. Of the two tributaries, the South Fork Crooked River is the larger and is sometimes considered part of the Crooked River proper. A variant name of the South Fork Crooked River is simply \"Crooked River\". The Deschutes River flows north into the Columbia River."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Smith_Rock_and_the_Crooked_River.jpg/330px-Smith_Rock_and_the_Crooked_River.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crooked_River_(Oregon)","wikidata":"Q535450"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River","wikidata":"Q4022"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Crooked_river_near_Post.jpg/500px-Crooked_river_near_Post.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Crooked_river_near_Post.jpg/1280px-Crooked_river_near_Post.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Crooked_river_near_Post.jpg/500px-Crooked_river_near_Post.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Upper river near Post","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrooked_river_near_Post.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Lower_Crooked_Wild_and_Scenic_River_Recreation_Area%2C_Oregon.jpg/500px-Lower_Crooked_Wild_and_Scenic_River_Recreation_Area%2C_Oregon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Lower_Crooked_Wild_and_Scenic_River_Recreation_Area%2C_Oregon.jpg/1280px-Lower_Crooked_Wild_and_Scenic_River_Recreation_Area%2C_Oregon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=